\section{Format Independence: Paired Constrained vs.\ Unconstrained Comparison}
\label{app:format-independence}

To test whether the BCS--accuracy relationship depends on the output format, we run both constrained (enum-based) and unconstrained (free-text) decoding on the same 25 batch type sets from the FewRel 80-relation pool (13 low-BCS, 12 high-BCS; count 8). Each batch uses identical instances and type sets; only the decoding format differs.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Metric} & \textbf{Constrained} & \textbf{Unconstrained} \\
\midrule
Mean accuracy & 79.5\% & 79.6\% \\
Pearson $r$ (BCS vs.\ acc) & $-$0.448 ($p = 0.025$) & $-$0.439 ($p = 0.028$) \\
Spearman $\rho$ (BCS vs.\ acc) & $-$0.157 ($p = 0.455$) & $-$0.144 ($p = 0.492$) \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{l}{\textit{Paired Fisher $z$-test for correlation equality}} \\
\quad Pearson $z$ & \multicolumn{2}{c}{$-$0.04 ($p = 0.97$)} \\
\quad Spearman $z$ & \multicolumn{2}{c}{$-$0.04 ($p = 0.97$)} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Paired comparison of constrained vs.\ unconstrained decoding on 25 identical FewRel batch type sets. Both formats yield virtually identical BCS--accuracy correlations (Fisher $z \approx 0$), confirming the confusability effect is format-independent. Mean accuracy is also unchanged ($\Delta = -0.08$\,pp), ruling out a format-driven accuracy shift.}
\label{tab:format_independence}
\end{table}

The near-zero Fisher $z$ statistics ($|z| < 0.05$, $p > 0.96$) confirm that the BCS--accuracy correlation is indistinguishable between formats. Both Pearson $r$ values are significant ($p < 0.03$), reflecting the bimodal BCS distribution of extreme batches (13 low + 12 high); the monotonic Spearman $\rho$ is modest and non-significant under both formats, consistent with the typical-batch BCS effect size (${\sim}2$\,pp; \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}). This result establishes that confusability-driven degradation is an intrinsic property of type semantic overlap, not an artifact of forced-choice decoding.

